{"benchmark": "autoregressive", "args": {"family": "gemma4", "model": "google/gemma-4-26B-A4B-it", "device": "auto", "dtype": "bfloat16", "compile": "auto", "expert_parallel": true, "experts_implementation": "by_token_count", "batch_sizes": [16, 2, 8], "variant_batch_size": 1, "variants": ["grouped"], "prompts": "chat", "prompt_length": 64, "prompt_bucket": 64, "max_new_tokens": 512, "stop_check_interval": 16, "warmup_runs": 2, "timed_runs": 3, "gsm8k_examples": 0, "gsm8k_batch_size": 10, "seed": 0, "label": "gemma4_b16", "output": "/opt/dgemma/repo/results/session2/results.jsonl", "batch_size": 16, "sampler": "static", "cache": "static", "max_denoising_steps": null, "attn_implementation": null, "do_sample": false}, "environment": {"torch": "2.15.0+cpu", "transformers": "5.22.2", "python": "3.12.16", "device": "tpu", "timestamp": "2026-11-10T11:55:13Z", "torch_tpu": "unknown", "device_count": 1}, "load_seconds": 14.259792925000056, "warmup_seconds": [90.31216635800001, 23.261561798999992], "median_tokens_per_second": 356.7062674945686, "median_total_seconds": 23.079171326999926, "peak_memory_bytes": 33540703232, "runs": [{"num_decode_steps": 512, "num_stop_checks": 31, "prefill_seconds": 0.08047976500006371, "total_seconds": 23.04615417699995, "generated_tokens": 8192, "decoded_tokens": 8192, "tokens_per_second": 356.7062674945686, "decoded_tokens_per_second": 356.7062674945686, "seconds_per_step": 0.044854832835937275, "token_checksum": 472087728, "sample_text": "To understand how a hash map handles collisions, we must first understand why they happen. \n\nA hash map uses a **hash function** to turn a key (like a string) into an integer (an index). Because the number of possible keys is usually much larger than the size of the array (the \"buckets\"), two different keys will eventually hash to the same index. This event is called a **collision**.\n\nHere is an explanation of the two primary strategies for handling these collisions and how they impact performance.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Collision Resolution Strategies\n\n#### A. Separate Chaining (Open Hashing)\nIn this method, each \"bucket\" in the array does not store a single value, but rather a pointer to a secondary data structure (usually a **Linked List**).\n\n*   **How it works:** When a collision occurs, the new key-value pair is simply appended to the list at that index.\n*   **Example:** If \"Alice\" and \"Bob\" both hash to index 5, index 5 will contain a list: `[Alice] -> [Bob]`.\n*   **Modern Optimization:** Many modern implementations (like Java 8+) use **Balanced Binary Search Trees** instead of linked lists once a bucket reaches a certain size. This prevents the list from becoming too long.\n\n#### B. Open Addressing (Closed Hashing)\nIn this method, all elements are stored directly within the array itself. If a collision occurs, the map looks for the next available \"empty\" slot according to a specific probe sequence.\n\n*   **Linear Probing:** If index $i$ is full, try $i+1$, then $i+2$, and so on, u"}, {"num_decode_steps": 512, "num_stop_checks": 31, "prefill_seconds": 0.32731608099993537, "total_seconds": 23.292338243000017, "generated_tokens": 8192, "decoded_tokens": 8192, "tokens_per_second": 356.7163986262201, "decoded_tokens_per_second": 356.7163986262201, "seconds_per_step": 0.04485355891015641, "token_checksum": 472087728, "sample_text": "To understand how a hash map handles collisions, we must first understand why they happen. \n\nA hash map uses a **hash function** to turn a key (like a string) into an integer (an index). Because the number of possible keys is usually much larger than the size of the array (the \"buckets\"), two different keys will eventually hash to the same index. This event is called a **collision**.\n\nHere is an explanation of the two primary strategies for handling these collisions and how they impact performance.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Collision Resolution Strategies\n\n#### A. Separate Chaining (Open Hashing)\nIn this method, each \"bucket\" in the array does not store a single value, but rather a pointer to a secondary data structure (usually a **Linked List**).\n\n*   **How it works:** When a collision occurs, the new key-value pair is simply appended to the list at that index.\n*   **Example:** If \"Alice\" and \"Bob\" both hash to index 5, index 5 will contain a list: `[Alice] -> [Bob]`.\n*   **Modern Optimization:** Many modern implementations (like Java 8+) use **Balanced Binary Search Trees** instead of linked lists once a bucket reaches a certain size. This prevents the list from becoming too long.\n\n#### B. Open Addressing (Closed Hashing)\nIn this method, all elements are stored directly within the array itself. If a collision occurs, the map looks for the next available \"empty\" slot according to a specific probe sequence.\n\n*   **Linear Probing:** If index $i$ is full, try $i+1$, then $i+2$, and so on, u"}, {"num_decode_steps": 512, "num_stop_checks": 31, "prefill_seconds": 0.10033041700000922, "total_seconds": 23.079171326999926, "generated_tokens": 8192, "decoded_tokens": 8192, "tokens_per_second": 356.5018806686203, "decoded_tokens_per_second": 356.5018806686203, "seconds_per_step": 0.04488054865234359, "token_checksum": 472087728, "sample_text": "To understand how a hash map handles collisions, we must first understand why they happen. \n\nA hash map uses a **hash function** to turn a key (like a string) into an integer (an index). Because the number of possible keys is usually much larger than the size of the array (the \"buckets\"), two different keys will eventually hash to the same index. This event is called a **collision**.\n\nHere is an explanation of the two primary strategies for handling these collisions and how they impact performance.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Collision Resolution Strategies\n\n#### A. Separate Chaining (Open Hashing)\nIn this method, each \"bucket\" in the array does not store a single value, but rather a pointer to a secondary data structure (usually a **Linked List**).\n\n*   **How it works:** When a collision occurs, the new key-value pair is simply appended to the list at that index.\n*   **Example:** If \"Alice\" and \"Bob\" both hash to index 5, index 5 will contain a list: `[Alice] -> [Bob]`.\n*   **Modern Optimization:** Many modern implementations (like Java 8+) use **Balanced Binary Search Trees** instead of linked lists once a bucket reaches a certain size. This prevents the list from becoming too long.\n\n#### B. Open Addressing (Closed Hashing)\nIn this method, all elements are stored directly within the array itself. If a collision occurs, the map looks for the next available \"empty\" slot according to a specific probe sequence.\n\n*   **Linear Probing:** If index $i$ is full, try $i+1$, then $i+2$, and so on, u"}]}
